==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈneɪmli/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-namely.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪmli|s=2}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|namely}}, {{m|enm|nameliche}}, {{m|enm|namelike}}, equivalent to {{af|en|name|-ly|id2=adverbial}}. Cognate with {{cog|stq|nämmelk}}, {{cog|fy|nammentlik}}, {{cog|nl|namelijk}}, {{cog|nds-de|nämlich}}, {{cog|de|nämlich}}, {{cog|da|nemlig}}, {{cog|sv|nämligen}}, {{cog|is|nefnilega}}.

====Adverb====
{{en-adv|-}}

# [[specifically|Specifically]]; [[that is to say]].
#: {{ux|en|Some of the students &mdash; '''namely''' Paul, Alice and Jake &mdash; seem to have trouble with geometry.}}
#: {{ux|en|There are three ways to do it, '''namely''' the right way, the wrong way and the Army way.}}
#* {{RQ:Orczy Miss Elliott|chapter=The Tragedy in Dartmoor Terrace|passage=“The story of this adoption is, of course, the pivot round which all the circumstances of the mysterious tragedy revolved. Mrs. Yule had an only son, '''namely''', William, to whom she was passionately attached ; but, like many a fond mother, she had the desire of mapping out that son's future entirely according to her own ideas.{{nb...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|now|rare}} <!--obsolete?--> [[especially|Especially]], [[above all]].
#* {{RQ:Mlry MrtDrthr|10.11|2|VIII|xj|passage=THus was sir Tramtryst longe there wel cherysshed / with the kynge and the quene / and '''namely''' with la beale Isoud / So vpon a daye / the quene and la beale Isoud made a bayne for syre Tramtryst / And whan he was in his bayne / the quene and Isoud her doughter romed vp & doune in the chamber}}
<!--Prior quote:#*:Thus was Sir Tramtryste longe there well cherysshed with the kynge and wyth the quene&mdash;and '''namely''' with La Beale Isode.-->

=====Usage notes=====
A synonymous expression is the use of colon—":", as in "There are three ways to do it: the right way, the wrong way{{nb...}}."

Since this is considered a [[dependent clause]], a '''comma''' should follow the expression and either a '''semicolon''' or a '''comma''' should precede it, depending on the strength of the break in continuity. "Namely" can thus almost be considered a conjunction.

